# Two PowerPC rotate-and-mask handlers: write the REPAIRS, because my sketches were rejected

VERDICT line first, then the liveness token `CODEX_PPCROT_20260910`.

**Please do not simply ratify.** A recent panel here had seven of nine seats confirm a premise the brief
stated wrongly. **Check the premises, not just the derivations** — every load-bearing claim below is
written as a question for that reason. And this brief exists BECAUSE a design review of yours already
rejected my proposed repair for both of these as insufficient; I do not want a second insufficient sketch,
I want the repair.

Ordinary correctness engineering on an instruction interpreter in an emulator written in C99. The tree is
`C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\GXEMUL-SEC\src\cpus\cpu_ppc_instr.c`; three unrelated 2021-vintage copies of
`src/` sit elsewhere under the project root and will give you line numbers off by hundreds.

## SETTLED PREMISES (established, cite rather than re-derive)

* A sibling handler in this same file, `X(rldicl)`, was shipped fixed today as `#522`: its mask was being
  accumulated into the ROTATED value and ANDed with a variable that was never anything but zero, so the
  destination was set to ZERO on every execution. Witnessed at rung 3 on unmodified `-E bareppc`,
  190-cell census, 24-row detector, nine mutants, zero escapes.
* **`DYNTRANS_DUALMODE_32` means this file is compiled TWICE**, and both PowerPC gates drive a 32-bit part
  selected explicitly in their probes. Gate 9 CONSTRUCTS 64-bit PowerPC CPUs (three machine subtypes
  default to the PPC970) but is a construction check with **no result oracle**. So no gate in this tree
  checks any 64-bit instruction result. That is why these two have gone unnoticed.
* `MODE_uint_t` is `uint64_t` in the 64-bit compilation and `uint32_t` in the 32-bit one.
* `_scratchpad/` holds four local PowerPC manuals as extracted text — `ppc_oea_book3_v201.txt`,
  `ppc_pem32_rev2_2001.txt`, `ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt`, `ppc_vea_book2_v201.txt`. **Cite only what you
  have read in those files**; this project has shipped page numbers that came from model output rather
  than a manual.

## SITE 1 — `X(rldimi)`: the mask always runs to bit 63

```c
X(rldimi)
{
	uint32_t iw = ic->arg[0];
	int rs = (iw >> 21) & 31, ra = (iw >> 16) & 31;
	int sh = ((iw >> 11) & 31) | ((iw & 2) << 4);
	int mb = ((iw >> 6) & 31) | (iw & 0x20);
	int rc = ic->arg[0] & 1;
	int m;
	uint64_t tmp, s = cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[rs];
	/*  TODO: Fix this, its performance is awful:  */
	while (sh-- != 0) {
		int b = (s >> 63) & 1;
		s = (s << 1) | b;
	}
	m = mb; tmp = 0;
	do {
		tmp |= ((uint64_t)1 << (63-m));
		m ++;
	} while (m != 63 - sh);
	cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra] &= ~tmp;
	cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra] |= (tmp & s);
	if (rc)
		update_cr0(cpu, cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra]);
}
```

**MY READING, WHICH I WANT CHECKED RATHER THAN CONFIRMED.** `while (sh-- != 0)` post-decrements, so `sh`
is **`-1` when the loop exits — including when `sh` was 0 and the body never ran.** The termination test is
therefore always `m != 64`, so the mask is always bits `mb..63` (PowerPC numbering) rather than
`mb..63-SH`, and `rldimi` **overwrites the SH destination bits it is required to preserve.**

**Questions, and the last two are the ones my rejected sketch missed:**

1. Is `sh == -1` on exit for **every** SH including 0? Is the rotate loop itself (`b = (s>>63)&1;
   s = (s<<1)|b;`) a correct ROTL64?
2. What does the manual's RTL actually say — quote it from one of the four local `.txt` files with its
   line number. Is the mask `MASK(MB, 63-SH)`, and is `MASK` **inclusive of both endpoints**?
3. The `do {...} while (m != X)` form is **exclusive of `m == X`**. So even with SH saved correctly, does
   the loop stop one bit SHORT? State what `X` must be.
4. **`MASK(x,y)` in this architecture WRAPS when `x > y`.** `MB=40, SH=40` gives `63-SH = 23 < MB`. What
   does the corrected `do/while` do at that encoding — and is it UB (`1 << (63-m)` with `m > 63`), an
   infinite loop, or fine? **This is the case my sketch had no answer for.** Is that encoding legal, or is
   it reserved/undefined so a guard is the right disposition?
5. **Write the repair.** Whole function, compiling C99, matching this file's idiom. If it needs four
   coordinated changes, make all four and say what each is for. Keep the performance TODO honest: I am not
   asking you to optimise, only to be correct.

## SITE 2 — `X(rlwimi)`: a 32-bit rotate performed at 64-bit width

```c
X(rlwimi)
{
	MODE_uint_t tmp = reg(ic->arg[0]), ra = reg(ic->arg[1]);
	uint32_t iword = ic->arg[2];
	int sh = (iword >> 11) & 31;
	int mb = (iword >> 6) & 31;
	int me = (iword >> 1) & 31;
	int rc = iword & 1;

	tmp = sh ? ((tmp << sh) | (tmp >> (32-sh))) : tmp;

	for (;;) {
		uint64_t mask;
		mask = (uint64_t)1 << (31-mb);
		ra &= ~mask;
		ra |= (tmp & mask);
		if (mb == me)
			break;
		mb ++;
		if (mb == 32)
			mb = 0;
	}
	reg(ic->arg[1]) = ra;
	if (rc)
		update_cr0(cpu, ra);
}
```

Its two siblings in the same file both truncate first and this one never does:

```c
X(rlwinm) { uint32_t tmp, iword = ic->arg[2]; ... tmp = (uint32_t)cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[rs];
            tmp = sh ? ((tmp << sh) | (tmp >> (32-sh))) : tmp; tmp &= (uint32_t)ic->arg[1];
            reg(ic->arg[0]) = tmp; }
X(rlwnm)  { /* identical shape, sh from a register */ }
```

**Questions:**

6. In the 64-bit compilation, `tmp` is 64 bits, so `tmp >> (32-sh)` brings **high-half** source bits down
   into the low 32. Is that right, and does the low-32 mask let those bits reach the destination?
7. What does the manual say `ROTL32` means in the 64-bit ISA — quote it with a line number. My
   understanding is `ROTL64(x32 || x32, n)`, i.e. the 32-bit value **duplicated**. If so, does the
   duplication matter here given `MASK(MB+32, ME+32)` confines the mask to the low 32?
8. **Does `rlwimi` in 64-bit mode leave RA's HIGH 32 bits unchanged?** The loop only clears bits inside
   the low 32, so this code preserves them. Confirm from the manual, because if the architecture instead
   requires something else the repair is bigger than a cast.
9. **Write the repair**, and say plainly whether it is the one-token `(uint32_t)` cast that makes it match
   its siblings, or more. Does `rlwinm`'s own idiom have a defect this would inherit?
10. `mb`/`me` come from a guest instruction word and the `for(;;)` walks `mb` around to `me`. Can any
    encoding make that loop not terminate? Show the argument, do not assert it.

## THE LAST TWO QUESTIONS, AND THEY DECIDE HOW THESE SHIP

